By Sok Pheannaka, Pun Niza, and Ly Sokna
A daily work commute became a traumatic experience in March 2025 for teacher Sum Samoeun, 46, when a motorbike crashed into him while travelling on Hun Sen Boulevard, also known as 60-Meter road, a two-lane southbound road.  
Samoeun was travelling from his home in Phnom Penh’s Dangkao district to Kandal’s Takhmao city with his wife and their three-year-old daughter when the motorbike crashed into them from behind.
While new road constructions in the city such as the 60-Meter road would be fitted with median barriers to reduce crashes, observers say they have little remedy for accidents caused by speeding and careless driving. 
Samoeun said the rider responsible for the crash had reportedly been leaving an entertainment club between 8 am and 9 am after a late shift as a security guard.
“When we fell, my motorbike landed on the​ side, while the security guard's motorbike ended up in the middle of the road,” he recalled, adding that fortunately there were no cars at the time.
The collision left Samoeun with chest pain and injuries to his shoulder and forearm. His wife and daughter were also injured.
“The accident happened because the security guard rode at high speed and might not have been fully conscious while driving on the road,” he said. 
Beyond the physical injuries, the accident left Samoeun traumatised, as he stated that even though he is careful while driving, accidents can happen in the blink of an eye. 
Although there is no official number of accidents on this road, there have been a number of high-profile crashes. In early 2025, Cambodian-French rapper Ruthko was killed in an accident after being hit by a Lexus car driver around 1 am. Due to the nature of the quiet road late at night, drivers often speed leading to fatal accidents. This was the case in 2024 in one accident that resulted in three deaths — a man and his two children — and seriously injuring his wife.
Traffic accidents have been deemed one of the leading causes of deaths in Cambodia, according to UNDP’s report on Road Traffic Accidents 2024. It said road accidents alone accounted for 5.4 deaths daily, with the capital charting the most number compared to the provinces. 
Hun Sen Boulevard is called 60-Meter road for its width. With a total construction cost of approximately $76 million, the road is considered a high-potential investment area, with numerous construction projects currently underway. 60-Meter road serves thousands of motorists every day, making it an essential connection.
