##Separating physical barriers for motorists
Hun Sen Boulevard did not have median barriers separating the two traffic sides previously. In fact, in 2017, former Prime Minister Hun Sen said the road could serve as a “landing strip” for certain types of aircrafts in the event of an emergency at Phnom Penh International Airport. 
However, after numerous accidents as well as concerns from related parties, the Phnom Penh Department of Public Works and Transport installed median barriers on February 6, 2025, to improve road safety and traffic flow.
The upgrade was divided into two sections. Metal barriers were installed from the Morodok Techo Flyover to the traffic light intersection at Hun Neang Boulevard, where the maximum speed limit was set at 40 km/h. From that intersection to National Road 2, concrete median barriers were installed, with a maximum speed limit of 60 km/h.
The project also included road markings separating car lanes from motorcycle lanes and installation of new traffic signs to guide motorists and organise traffic flow.
Despite these changes, questions remain about whether the road design and traffic enforcement could safeguard road users, including cyclists and pedestrians. 
While lane separation is intended to improve safety, its effectiveness relies on whether drivers stick to the designated lanes and speed limit. It also depends on whether traffic laws are regularly enforced. 
Yong Kim Eng, President of the People's Centre for Development and Peace, said that installing physical barriers could further prevent drivers from crossing into the wrong lane and help reduce disruptions and traffic congestion. 
“Physical barriers separating lanes for different types of vehicles can greatly increase the safety for people on the road,” he said. 
However, traffic law enforcement remains a challenge, as some drivers continue to disregard designated lanes and traffic rules, he mentioned. Motorcycles sometimes use car lanes, while cars may also enter lanes designated for motorcycles, making it more difficult to maintain proper traffic flow.
Seng Reaksmey, a 42-year-old vendor selling drinks along 60-Meter, said traffic is heavy as the road is spacious. 
“The road is big enough, but congestion rarely happens,” he said, adding that motorists drive well and maintain moderate speed. Nevertheless, he observed that motorcycles often drive in the car lane and at high speed. 
When asked about installing physical barriers, Reaksmey said separate car and motorcycle lanes could make travel less convenient as it reduces the available road space. Despite this, he felt that separating lanes for different types of vehicles could improve road safety. 
Kiripost reached out to Phan Rim, Spokesperson for the Ministry of Public Works and Transport (MPWT), but was referred to Chev Hak, Deputy Commissioner General of the National Police. Hak did not respond at the time of publication.
Dao Samphors, Spokesperson for Phnom Penh City Hall, could not be reached for comment on whether Phnom Penh City Hall and MPWT would install physical barriers. 
##Calls for stricter enforcement
In the first six months of 2026, traffic accidents increased by 213 cases or 20 percent to 1,279 cases compared to 2025. The death toll also grew 11 percent to 773, while injuries rose by 468 cases to 1,944, equivalent to 32 percent, according to data from the Ministry of Interior.
The main causes of the accidents include speeding, failure to yield right of way, overtaking, careless turning, drink-driving, and ignoring traffic signals. 
Kim Eng of People's Centre for Development and Peace opined that two major contributors are “driving under the influence and speeding”, calling for strict law enforcement. “If motorists break the law, there should be consequences. If there are no fines or other legal action, they will continue to break the law.”
Kim Eng stressed that behaviour changes, including improved driving conditions and road enforcement, would support the effectiveness of infrastructure like lane separators. 
Kiripost contacted Chuon Narin, Deputy Commissioner General and Phnom Penh Municipal Police Chief, via WhatsApp for comment about the establishment of a mobile enforcement team, but he did not respond. 
The mobile enforcement team is expected to check on blood alcohol levels of motorists and crackdown on speeding and people driving under the influence. 
Tha Sileng, a resident of Dangkao district who uses the 60-Meter road daily, told Kiripost that the road is slippery, requiring her to be extra careful when driving. 
“The road is slippery when it rains,” she said, adding that she has seen only a few police patrolling the roads. 
“I request the deployment of traffic police to help manage traffic flow and take action against those who exceed the speed limit.”
Sileng said the presence of police patrolling the roads encourages drivers to be more cautious and avoid driving above the speed limit, which could help reduce traffic accidents. 
"It is also a good thing that the government has installed barriers for motorcycles,” she said.
